Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Document observed VPE echo runner level tuning

Record optional threshold and normalization flags from the audible hardware test without claiming universal gain settings or verified stop-word cancellation.

Co-authored-by: Copilot App <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 examples/voice_call/README.md | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/examples/voice_call/README.md b/examples/voice_call/README.md
index 6fa839c3bd..80d628b579 100644
--- a/examples/voice_call/README.md
+++ b/examples/voice_call/README.md
@@ -36,6 +36,21 @@ accepts 48 kHz signed 16-bit little-endian stereo PCM through its stock 32-bit
 I2S slots. The runner must use this advertised format (the echo runner converts
 its captured audio); there is no on-device resampler in this prototype.
 
+### Echo runner levels on stock VPE
+
+For the Wyoming echo runner, the optional `--speech-threshold 10 --normalize`
+flags made the hardware demo audible without changing firmware or increasing
+the DAC volume from `0.5`. In that test, the call's gain-1 microphone source
+produced speech RMS values around 16-23 and background RMS around 1-2; the
+runner's threshold of 500 never detected a sentence. Normalization limited the
+output peak to 8192 (approximately -12 dBFS), and the user heard their sentence
+returned through Home Assistant.
+
+These are observations from one device and room, not universal microphone
+settings or an automatic gain-control recommendation. Measure the input levels
+before changing the threshold or playback level. This confirms audible echo,
+not stop-wake-word cancellation, which still needs a separate hardware check.
+
 ## Component contract
 
 `voice_call` references a microphone source, a dedicated standard `i2s_audio`
